{"id":"dd339e27-2631-41ec-8c8a-517efbc85426","object":"chat.completion","created":1785009247,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"from collections import defaultdict\\n\\ndef solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    node_by_id = {node['id']: node for node in nodes}\\n    children = defaultdict(list)\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        parent = node['parent']\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            children[parent].append(node['id'])\\n    retained = set(node_by_id.keys())\\n    symbol_counts = defaultdict(int)\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        symbol_counts[node['symbol']] += 1\\n    for sym in required_symbols:\\n        if symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) == 0:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Required symbol '{sym}' not found in tree\\\")\\n    sorted_ids = sorted(node_by_id.keys(), key=lambda nid: (-node_by_id[nid]['depth'], nid))\\n    removed = []\\n    for nid in sorted_ids:\\n        node = node_by_id[nid]\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if any(c in retained for c in children.get(nid, [])):\\n            continue\\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols and symbol_counts[node['symbol']] <= 1:\\n            continue\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        symbol_counts[node['symbol']] -= 1\\n        removed.append(nid)\\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\n        'removed_node_ids': removed,\\n        'retained_node_ids': retained_ids\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to write solve(case) for task SE-PE-01. The task is \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\". Based on the description, we have a tree structure with nodes having id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. required_symbols is a list of symbols that must remain. The goal is to remove removable nodes while preserving required symbols, and also ensuring that the tree remains \"grammar-valid\" and preserves \"interestingness oracle\". The candidate_artifact description mentions \"Perses syntax-guided reduction\" and mechanisms like \"grammar_valid\", \"interestingness_preserved\", \"registered_candidate_order\", \"stable_ties\". But we don't have actual grammar or oracle. The fixture payloads show simple trees with depth up to 2. The required_symbols are \"root\" and \"s1\". So root is always required. The nodes have removable flag.\n\nWe need to decide which removable nodes to remove. The problem statement says: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\"\n\nSo the algorithm: iterate over nodes in order of depth descending (deeper first), and for nodes at same depth, by node ID (lexicographic? likely string order or insertion order? but ID is string like \"n1\", \"n2\", \"n6\". We'll treat as string natural order? The fixture shows \"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"n6\". So we can sort by depth descending, then by id (string). For each node, if removable, check if it can be removed without removing required symbols. But we also need to ensure that after removal, the remaining tree still preserves \"required_symbols\". Since required symbols are symbols that must appear at least once, we need to check that after removing this node, there is still at least one node for each required symbol. Also we need to consider children: \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" So if the node has children that are retained, we cannot remove it. That makes sense: removing a node would delete its subtree, so we must ensure that no children are retained (i.e., all its descendants are also removed). So we need to process in post-order (deepest first) so that we remove descendant nodes first, then possibly parent. This matches \"deeper before shallower\". We'll maintain a set of retained nodes initially all nodes (since we start with no removals). Then iterate nodes in desired order, and for each node, if removable and not required symbol (but root is also not removable), check if removing it would still have all required symbols present in the retained set (excluding this node and its descendants). And also check that its children (if any) are not retained. Since we process deepest first, by the time we consider a parent, its children have already been considered for removal. If any child is still retained, we cannot remove the parent. But parental removal would also remove children? Actually if we remove a parent, we must also remove all its descendants because it's a tree. But the algorithm description: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" And \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" So we treat removal as cutting the entire subtree. So we must ensure that after removing that node and its subtree, required symbols are still present elsewhere. And we must also ensure that the node's children are not retained because if they were retained, removal would be contradictory. So algorithm: Build a mapping from parent to children. Start with retained = set of all node ids. Then process nodes in order depth descending, id ascending. For each node, check if it is removable and not the root (since root not removable by flag). Also check if the node's symbol is not mandatory? Actually required symbols are symbols that must exist somewhere. So we need to check if the node is the only node providing that symbol; if so, can't remove. So for each required symbol, we need to know if there are other nodes (outside this node's subtree) that have that symbol. Since we are processing top-down? But deeper first means we may remove descendants before checking parent. We need to maintain a count of nodes per symbol overall. Initially, count per symbol of all nodes. When we consider removing a node, we need to know if after removing that node and its entire subtree, the count for each required symbol remains >=1. This requires knowing the subtree's symbol counts. So we precompute subtree symbol counts (or just check if that node is the only contributor for any required symbol). For efficiency, we can precompute for each node the set of required symbols that are exclusively present in its subtree. But given small trees, we can just simulate removal by temporarily removing the node and its descendants from retained set, check required symbols, then addback if not removed. However, we need to ensure that we only remove if children are not retained. Since we process deepest first, children may have already been removed. So children retained set will be empty if they were removed. But we need to check that we are not removing a node whose children are still present in retained set. That is, if any child is in retained set, skip removal. That makes sense because child would be deeper and should have been considered first; if it was removable but not removed, maybe it was kept because it was required. In that case, we cannot remove parent. So we skip.\n\nThus algorithm:\n\n- Build adjacency: for each node, store children list.\n- Create dict mapping id to node info.\n- Build initial retained set = all node ids.\n- Compute initial per-symbol counts (number of nodes with that symbol). Required symbols is list.\n- Create list of node ids sorted by depth descending, then by id (string order).\n- Iterate through sorted list:\n    - node = nodes[id]\n    - if not node['removable']: continue\n    - if node['id'] == 'root'? Actually root not removable anyway.\n    - Check that all children are not in retained set (i.e., removed). If any child is still retained, cannot remove parent. Continue.\n    - Check that removing this node and its descendants (which are all already removed if we processed deepest first? But not necessarily; some descendants may be non-removable and thus still retained. Actually if a descendant is non-removable, we cannot remove its parent because that would remove non-removable node. The condition \"node with retained children\" covers that: if any child is retained (including non-removable), we cannot remove parent. So we check children first. So if any child is retained, skip.\n    - Now also need to ensure that required symbols are still present after removal. We can simulate by temporarily removing the node and its subtree from the symbol counts. Since we already know that all descendants are removed? Not necessarily. But we have ensured no children are retained, but descendants might be non-removable and retained? Actually if a child is retained, we skip. If child is not retained, that means it's removed. But descendant could be a grandchild that is non-removable? If child is removed, its entire subtree is removed with it. So we can assume that if all children are removed, then the entire subtree below this node is removed. Because children are roots of their subtrees. So after processing deepest first, if we remove a node, we will have removed its descendants first (if removable) or kept them if non-removable. But if a descendant is non-removable, then its parent (which is also descendant) cannot be removed because that non-removable node is a child of some node. So ultimately, if we reach a node and none of its children are retained, then the whole subtree below is removed (because children are no longer retained, meaning they were removed). But careful: If a child is non-removable and thus retained, then parent cannot be removed. So the condition holds. Therefore, when we check children, we guarantee that no descendant is retained. So the subtree is entirely absent from retained set. Therefore, removing this node essentially removes only itself from retained set (since subtree already removed). So the symbol counts: initially we have retained set including this node. Its symbol is count1. After removal, count for that symbol decreases by 1. We need to ensure that for each required symbol, the new count >=1. But also, we need to consider that required symbols might be present in other parts of tree. So we can compute before removal: for each required symbol, check if there is at least one node with that symbol outside this node's subtree. Since we already ensured subtree is empty of retained nodes, we just need to check that there is at least one other retained node (outside this node) with that symbol. Equivalent: if node's symbol is required and it is the only node with that symbol (count per symbol = 1), then cannot remove. Also if required symbol is not present elsewhere? Actually required symbols must appear at least once overall. So if node's symbol is required and count per symbol ==1, then cannot remove. If there are multiple nodes with that symbol, then OK.\n\nBut also required symbols list may contain symbols that are not present? Unlikely. But we should validate that all required symbols are present initially; otherwise raise ValueError.\n\nThus we need to maintain a count of nodes per symbol in the retained set. Initially, we have all nodes. As we process nodes and decide to remove, we update the count. Since we process deepest first, we know that when we consider a node, its subtree (if any) is already removed, so its symbol count already reflects removal of descendants? Actually descendants may have been removed earlier, so the count already decreased for those symbols. So we need a mutable count that we update as we remove nodes. So we can maintain a dict symbol -> count of retained nodes. Initially compute from all nodes. Then as we remove nodes (which we decide), we decrement the count for that node's symbol. For a parent node, when we consider removal, the descendants are already removed, so their counts already decreased. So the current count only includes retained nodes (the node itself and any ancestors/other branches). So we can simply check if after decrementing, all required symbols have count >=1. So we can do a test: if node['removable'] and node['id'] != root (but root not removable anyway) and all children are not in retained set, then:\n    - if node['symbol'] in required_symbols and current_count[node['symbol']] == 1: cannot remove (would become 0).\n    - else, remove: remove node from retained set, decrement count, add to removed list.\n\nBut is that sufficient? Consider required symbol \"s1\". The root has symbol \"root\". n1 has \"s1\", n4 also has \"s1\". So initially count[\"s1\"]=2 (n1 and n4). If we remove n1, count becomes 1. OK. If we remove n4, count becomes 1. But can we remove both? If we remove n1 first, then count[\"s1\"]=1, then when we consider n4, it has symbol \"s1\", count=1, cannot remove. So that's correct.\n\nBut we also need to consider that required symbols may not be leaf nodes. The condition \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" As we described.\n\nNow, what about \"grammar_valid\" and \"interestingness_preserved\"? The candidate_artifact mentions these as hard contracts. But we don't have any oracle. The fixture does not provide any grammar or interestingness function. So likely the requirement is to simply follow the rule that we never remove a node that would break the symbiotic constraints derived from the structure. Since there's no additional constraints, we assume that as long as required symbols remain, and we don't remove a node with retained children, the grammar is valid. Maybe the grammar is that the tree is valid under some CFG, but we don't have productions. Possibly this simplified fixture expects us to just use the rules given in the task description: deeper first, stable ties by id, never remove root/required/with retained children, check that all required symbols remain. That's what we can implement.\n\nThe output must have removed_node_ids (list of IDs in removal order) and retained_node_ids (sorted list of retained IDs). So we accumulate removed ids in order we remove. Retained ids we can get from retained set after processing, sorted.\n\nEdge cases: Node with removable True but symbol required and count=1 -> cannot remove.\nNode with parent that is not root? The root is the only node with parent null.\nWe must also consider \"required_symbols\" list may include \"root\"? In fixtures, root is always included. So we treat root as required symbol.\n\nWe need to handle case where a required symbol is not present at all -> raise ValueError.\n\nAlso, we should validate that the tree structure is consistent? Not required, but we can assume.\n\nNow, write solve(case). The case is the payload object. It has \"nodes\" list and \"required_symbols\". We'll build a dict from id to node. Also build children map. We'll compute depths for each node? The node already has \"depth\" field. So we can sort by depth desc, then id.\n\nImplementation steps:\n1. Extract nodes = case['nodes']\n2. required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols']) (list)\n3. Build dict node_by_id = {node['id']: node for node in nodes}\n4. Build children: for node in nodes: parent = node['parent']; if parent is not None: children[parent].append(node['id'])\n5. Initialize retained = set(node_by_id.keys())\n6. Initialize symbol_counts = defaultdict(int); for node in nodes: symbol_counts[node['symbol']] += 1\n7. Validate that every required symbol is present in symbol_counts with count>=1; if not, raise ValueError.\n8. Create list of ids sorted: sorted(sorted(keys), key=lambda nid: (-node_by_id[nid]['depth'], nid))  # deeper first, then id string\n9. removed_ids = []\n10. For each nid in sorted_ids:\n    node = node_by_id[nid]\n    if not node['removable']: continue\n    # Check if any child still retained\n    children_retained = [c for c in children.get(nid, []) if c in retained]\n    if children_retained: continue\n    # Check if symbol is required and count is 1 -> cannot remove\n    if node['symbol'] in required_symbols and symbol_counts[node['symbol']] == 1:\n        continue\n    # Remove\n    retained.remove(nid)\n    symbol_counts[node['symbol']] -= 1\n    removed_ids.append(nid)\n11. retained_ids = sorted(retained)\n12. Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_ids, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_ids}\n\nTest on first fixture case:\nnodes with ids root, n1..n6. required: root, s1.\nRemovable: n2, n4, n6.\nDepth: root0, n1,n2,n3 depth1, n4,n5,n6 depth2.\nSorted by depth desc: depth2: n4,n5,n6. Then depth1: n1,n2,n3. Then depth0: root.\nBut n5 not removable, so skip.\nn4 removable, children? n4 has parent n2, no children (it's leaf). children list empty. Check symbol s1: symbol_counts initially: s1? n1 and n4 => count=2. Can remove n4? Yes. So remove n4.\nn6 removable, symbol s0. Count s0? nodes: n3 (s0)? Actually n3 symbol s0? n3 is depth1 symbol s0. n6 symbol s0? n6 is depth2 parent n1 symbol s0. So count s0 initially: n3 and n6 => 2. Remove n6, count s0 becomes1. OK.\nNow depth1: n1 not removable. n2 removable, children? n2 has child n4, but n4 was removed (not in retained). So children_retained empty. symbol s2? n2 symbol s2. Count s2? nodes: n2 and n5? n5 symbol s2? Actually n5 is depth2 parent n3 symbol s2. So count s2: n2 and n5 => 2. So can remove n2? Yes. Remove n2.\nn3 not removable.\nroot not removable.\nSo removed_ids = [n4, n6, n2]. retained_ids = [root, n1, n3, n5]? Check: n1 retained, n3 retained, n5 retained, root retained. That's 4. But also n2 removed, n4 removed, n6 removed. So retained = {root, n1, n3, n5}. Sorted: [\"n1\",\"n3\",\"n5\",\"root\"]? alphabetical: \"n1\",\"n3\",\"n5\",\"root\". That seems plausible.\n\nBut is that the intended? Hard to know. The fixture shows only one nonce; we can't verify. But matches logic.\n\nNow we need to output as JSON with key \"implementation\". The implementation must be Python source string. We'll write the function solve(case). We'll include imports (collections.defaultdict, maybe json for serialization? But function returns dict, no need json). We'll raise ValueError if required symbol missing.\n\nMake sure to handle case where root is not removable? root has removable=false anyway.\n\nOne nuance: The condition \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" We interpret it as \"a node that has any child that is still retained\". That's what we do.\n\nAlso \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" We sort by depth desc then id string. We'll use default string sort, which is fine.\n\nNow produce the JSON response with implementation key.\n\nWe need to escape the Python code as JSON string. We'll write the code and then wrap in a string with proper escaping.\n\nLet's write the Python code:\n\n```python\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\n    node_by_id = {node['id']: node for node in nodes}\n    children = defaultdict(list)\n    for node in nodes:\n        parent = node['parent']\n        if parent is not None:\n            children[parent].append(node['id'])\n    retained = set(node_by_id.keys())\n    symbol_counts = defaultdict(int)\n    for node in nodes:\n        symbol_counts[node['symbol']] += 1\n    # Validate required symbols present\n    for sym in required_symbols:\n        if symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) == 0:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Required symbol '{sym}' not found in tree\")\n    # Sort nodes: depth desc, then id string\n    sorted_ids = sorted(node_by_id.keys(), key=lambda nid: (-node_by_id[nid]['depth'], nid))\n    removed = []\n    for nid in sorted_ids:\n        node = node_by_id[nid]\n        if not node['removable']:\n            continue\n        # Check children retained\n        if any(c in retained for c in children.get(nid, [])):\n            continue\n        # Check if symbol required and would become absent\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols and symbol_counts[node['symbol']] <= 1:\n            continue\n        # Remove\n        retained.remove(nid)\n        symbol_counts[node['symbol']] -= 1\n        removed.append(nid)\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)\n    return {\n        'removed_node_ids': removed,\n        'retained_node_ids': retained_ids\n    }\n```\n\nWe need to ensure that when checking symbol count, we use current count (which includes the node itself). The check if count == 1 -> removal would make 0, so skip. We used <=1 to be safe.\n\nNow produce JSON: {\"implementation\": \"<escaped code>\"}\n\nWe'll escape newlines and quotes.\n\nLet's produce final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2169,"completion_tokens":4672,"total_tokens":6841,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":4311},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":121},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}